Keyword arguments in Python allow you to specify arguments by their parameter names, regardless of their order. This enhances code readability and flexibility.

There are two types of arguments in Python:

Positional arguments: Arguments that need to be in the same order as the parameters in the function definition.
Keyword arguments: Arguments that can be provided in any order by explicitly specifying the parameter name.
Consider the following example with keyword arguments:





def printItem(id, name, price):
    print("Id is", id)
    print("Name is", name)
    print("Price is", price)
​
printItem(id=101, name="abc", price=100)
print()
printItem(name="xyz", price=200, id=102)
print()
printItem(price=150, id=103, name="pqr")

Output
Id is 101
Name is abc
Price is 100

Id is 102
Name is xyz
Price is 200

Id is 103
Name is pqr
Price is 150
Explanation:
Arguments are passed by parameter names, so the order does not matter.
This makes the function calls more readable and flexible, especially for functions with many parameters.
Advantages of Using Keyword Arguments
You do not need to remember the order of parameters.
The code is more readable, explicitly stating which value corresponds to which parameter.
Allows skipping optional parameters easily without affecting other arguments.
Makes functions more flexible and reduces chances of errors when many parameters exist.
